                <h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>Localization is more than simple language translation. Users in Indonesia and Brazil have completely different payment habits, aesthetic preferences, and holiday shopping psychology. Many teams fail in Southeast Asia and LATAM not because of product issues, but because of localization oversights.</p>
<p>This article outlines 5 common pitfalls and provides an actionable SOP for operators to use before launching ads or during account optimization.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: 4 Hard Indicators to Check Before Launch</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Check Item</th>
<th>Southeast Asia</th>
<th>LATAM</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Language Variants</td>
<td>Indonesian ≠ Malay, Thai needs unique fonts</td>
<td>Brazilian Portuguese vs. European Spanish</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payment Adaptation</td>
<td>OVO/Dana/GoPay wallets</td>
<td>PIX/Boleto Bancário/local credit cards</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Holiday Nodes</td>
<td>Eid/Deepavali/Chinese New Year</td>
<td>Father's Day/Carnival/World Cup</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Landing Page Load</td>
<td>Mobile first screen &lt; 3 seconds</td>
<td>Must work on 2G/3G networks</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operator Tip</strong>: If you're still using Stripe only in Mexico, you'll likely lose 60%+ of conversions. Add OXXO before launching.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: 5 Common Material Localization Mistakes</h2>
<h3>Mistake 1: One-Size-Fits-All Creatives</h3>
<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Machine-translated English ads cause issues. A utility app in Thailand used "OK" hand gesture creatives, triggering user complaints and account suspension.</p>
<p><strong>Correct Approach</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Color preferences: Southeast Asia prefers bright/gold tones; LATAM prefers warm tones (orange, red)</li>
<li>Portrait style: Southeast Asian users accept realistic life scenes; LATAM users prefer expressive, exaggerated emotions</li>
<li>Taboo elements: Avoid pork/alcohol imagery in Islamic regions; avoid black cats/crows in LATAM</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mistake 2: Ignoring Language Variants</h3>
<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Indonesian users feel alienated by Malaysian language variants; Brazilian users find European Portuguese "unfriendly."</p>
<p><strong>Correct Approach</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Prioritize native speakers from target markets</li>
<li>Human校对 all key copy (CTAs, promotional info)</li>
<li>Avoid Chinese-style literal translations</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mistake 3: Inconsistent Ad-to-Landing Page Promise</h3>
<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Ad says "7-day free trial" but landing page says "50% off first month" — trust drops, conversion halves.</p>
<p><strong>Correct Approach</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep core selling points consistent between ad and landing page</li>
<li>Match price/currency units to target market (IDR in Indonesia, BRL/Peso in LATAM)</li>
<li>Form fields match local habits (Brazilians include birthday; LATAM users prefer WhatsApp)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mistake 4: Broken Payment Flow</h3>
<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Users abandon when local payment methods are unavailable. An e-commerce team had 1.2% conversion in Mexico with PayPal only; after adding OXXO, it jumped to 3.8%.</p>
<p><strong>Correct Approach</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Southeast Asia: Cover e-Wallet + bank transfer</li>
<li>LATAM: PIX (Brazil) / OXXO (Mexico) are standard</li>
<li>Show local logos and trust badges on payment page</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mistake 5: Ignoring Local Holiday Nodes</h3>
<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Running alcohol ads during Ramadan or dark-themed creatives during Carnival wastes budget on indifferent audiences.</p>
<p><strong>Correct Approach</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Plan holiday creatives 2 weeks in advance</li>
<li>Southeast Asia: Jan-Feb CNY, Apr-May Ramadan/Songkran, year-end shopping season</li>
<li>LATAM: Feb-Mar Carnival, June Father's Day, Nov-Dec Black Friday</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Pre-Launch Checklist</h2>
<pre><code>□ All copy reviewed by native speaker
□ Currency matches target market
□ Landing page loads &lt; 3 seconds (mobile)
□ Local payment methods configured
□ Holidays/cultural taboos confirmed
□ Ad account timezone matches market
□ Creatives passed platform review
□ Contact info complete (WhatsApp/local support)
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Post-Launch Localization Review</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Segment accounts by region</strong>: Don't lump all markets into one ad group; separate regions for clear data comparison</li>
<li><strong>Monitor CTR and CPA differences</strong>: If Indonesian CTR is normal but CPA is high, check landing page; if CTR itself is low, check creative localization</li>
<li><strong>Collect local user feedback</strong>: App reviews, support tickets, social comments reveal real pain points</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Localization is fundamental to overseas advertising, yet it's where most teams stumble. Following this SOP will help you avoid 80% of common mistakes. If you encounter other localization challenges, welcome to connect with Yiyu Media's team — we can help diagnose account issues and optimize your strategy.</p>
